WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.740
So, The New York Times has just published
its list of must-see places to go in 2019

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:18.880
and nestled quietly in the top 25 destinations
is none other than the Falkland Islands.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:25.590
We’re not talking tropical island paradise
here, we’re talking about a remote, archipelago

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:30.450
in the South Atlantic, more than 700 islands
exposed to the harsh elements, with a tiny

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.570
population of just over 3,000.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.920
A territory as empty as it is inhospitable.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.940
Nevertheless, this windblown backwater was
the cause of intense political tension in

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.620
the second half of the twentieth century,
culminating in a war between the United Kingdom

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:51.320
and Argentina.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:01:06.960
It’s important to understand here that for
Argentinians, the Falklands issue is a national

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.170
issue.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:12.020
Argentinian children learn very, very early
that the Falklands are a part of Argentina..

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.420
For them, the British “occupation” of
the islands was nothing more than imperial

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.610
plunder.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:26.080
“In peace with nobility and in peace with
honour.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:39.929
But we will also chase anyone who dares touch
a square metre of Argentine territory”

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.830
So given its controversial history, the New
York Times listsparked a few questions:.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.730
Do the Falkland Islands belong to Argentina?

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:50.150
And why are they so important to mainland
Argentinianss?

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.280
After all, Argentina is a huge country, the
eighth largest country in the world.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.430
So why all the fuss about this remote, inhospitable
and largely uninhabited archipelago?

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:02.060
Well, this video aims to answer these questions..

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:12.959
First things first, Let’s find out how it
all began…

00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:15.099
THE ORIGIN OF AN ENDLESS DISPUTE

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:18.599
The beginning of the nineteenth century.

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:22.099
The Spanish colonies in America begin to protest
against Spanish rule.

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:26.819
The recent United States Declaration of Independence
and the French Revolution have ignited a desire

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:30.580
for autonomy and freedom that cannot be suppressed.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:35.930
In 1808, the Napoleonic invasion of Spain
led to the dismantling of Madrid’s power

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.730
and that kicked off a race towards independence
that more or less resulted in Latin America

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.560
as we know it today.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:48.450
So it was that on May 25th, 1810, just two
years after the French occupation of Spain,

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.920
the municipal council of the City of Buenos
Aires decided to replace the Viceroy of the

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.590
Río de la Plata with the First Government
Junta.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.450
Argentinians today think of this as their
first patriotic government.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.079
Argentina was on its own road to revolution
but it wouldn’t be free of Spain until six

00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:05.079
years later.

00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:09.939
On July 9th, 1816 came the proclamation of
independence of the United Provinces of the

00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:11.239
Rio de la Plata.

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:15.690
And this is where the first confusion arises:

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:24.780
You see, many people think that the Viceroyalty
of the Rio de la Plata, created in 1776 by

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.560
King Carlos III of Spain, was synonymous with
what we know of today as Argentina... but

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.290
it wasn’t the case.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.290
Let me explain.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.829
While it’s true that the Viceroyalty of
the Río de la Plata included modern-day Argentina,

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:44.999
it also included Uruguay, Bolivia and Paraguay.

00:03:44.999 --> 00:03:49.260
Some of you must be thinking, “Hang on Simon,
where do the Falklands fit in with all of

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.260
this?”

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.930
Well the fact is that Uruguay, Paraguay and
Bolivia, along with the Falkland Islands,

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.900
were all part of the Viceroyalty of the Rio
de la Plata…

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.520
The thing is, I don’t think that you’ll
find many Argentinians claiming sovereignty

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.829
over Paraguay, Bolivia or Uruguay these days.

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:06.989
And of course if they did, everyone would
think they were crazy.

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:08.900
So why the Falklands?

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.459
The fact that the Falklands were part of this
mammoth Viceroyalty which belonged to the

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:13.870
Spanish crown is not surprising.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:19.340
What is surprising is Argentina’s claim
on the Falklands, especially given that, until

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:23.450
this territory was consolidated as a British
sovereignty, it was passed back and forth

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:29.450
between European powers numerous times.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:36.220
(FROM HAND TO HAND, FROM PLAY TO PLAY)

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:43.280
The history of the Falkland Islands is actually
much simpler and shorter than some may think.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.060
You see, until the mid 1700s the FalklandIslands
weren’t even inhabited on a permanent basis.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:53.639
And the first people to live there permanently
were not the British or the Spanish, but the

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:54.639
French.

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:56.960
Yes, you heard that correctly, the French.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.750
But, that’s only the start.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:06.620
Between 1764 and 1833, the islands passed
from one nation to another:

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:11.080
first from France to Spain, then to England,
then backto Spain and finally, since 1833,

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:18.180
the Falkland have been under British sovereignty.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.770
So let’s do some quick addition.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:23.590
Since they began to be permanently occupied,
the Falklands have been under Spanish sovereignty

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:28.280
for just over 40 years and under British sovereignty
for more than 180 years.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.550
So why does Argentina stake its claim so vigorously?

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:36.479
Well it all goes back to the time between
the May Revolution and the subsequent declaration

00:05:36.479 --> 00:05:39.630
of independence of the United Provinces of
the Rio de la Plata.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.669
At that time, the local authorities began
sending expeditions to the Falkland islands,

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:45.460
which they considered their own.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.889
And this is precisely where the controversy
arose.

00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:53.099
Because while the Argentinian version insists
that the English snatched part of their territory

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:58.970
in 1833, the truth is that, at that time Argentina
was still a work in progress and its borders

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:00.830
were far from defined.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.389
Not convinced?

00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:03.389
Check this out:

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:07.870
Just five years before the British returned
to the Falklands in 1833, Argentina lost Uruguay;

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:12.740
What’s more, it took until 1862, that’s
almost three decades later, for Argentina

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:19.949
to have a national president.

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:24.889
In other words, in 1833 when the English regained
control over the Falklands, Argentina’s

00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:28.860
borders were yet to be decided, there was
no Argentinian national government, and so

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.759
of course they didn’t have anything like
a national army.

00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:36.379
In fact, it may seem hard to believe, but
between the years 1852 and 1860, the state

00:06:36.379 --> 00:06:40.789
of Buenos Aires wasn’t even part of the
Argentine Confederation.

00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:45.759
But in the grand scheme of things, this isn’t
so extraordinary.

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:48.900
Stories rely on continuous changes in the
rules of the game.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:54.140
Viewed through a historical prism, sovereignties
come and go all the time.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:07:00.699
Now, it’s clear that history can be framed
and interpreted in many different ways.

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:04.819
And that may explain why many Argentinians
insist that when the English reasserted control

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:09.919
over the Falklands in 1833, the islands were
already a part of Argentina as we know it

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:13.379
today and therefore, part of the Argentinian
homeland.

00:07:13.379 --> 00:07:18.550
But, let's see,190 years later and can you
guesshow many people of Argentinian origin

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:20.669
live in the Falkland Islands today?

00:07:20.669 --> 00:07:21.830
Only 18.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:27.020
In fact, there are almost ten times as many
inhabitants from Chile as there are from Argentina.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:36.050
Nevertheless, not so long ago, the contested
sovereignty was grounds for war.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:42.110
(AN UNEXPECTED WAR)

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:47.870
Ever since the British regained control of
the Falkland Islands, Argentina has wanted

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:48.870
them back.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:51.320
It’s been like a national obsession through
the generations.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:57.300
In 1888 they began publishing maps that included
these previously disregarded islands within

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.210
Argentina’s borders.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:04.000
Now let’s just be clear that, apart from
the map redesign, there were no great claims

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.750
on the Falklands from either side until the
Second World War.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.949
Up until then, Argentina and England were
two friendly allies.

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:14.150
In fact, the Latin American country that received
the most direct and indirect investments from

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:16.050
England was Argentina.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:20.199
But... after the Second World War...

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:21.389
Everything changed.

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:24.990
For one thing, commercial relations between
the countries were no longer as significant.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:28.900
Secondly, the election of General Perón as
Argentina’s president gave weight to his

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.270
nationalist and anti-imperialist discourse.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:36.320
And thirdly, the newly created United Nations
was focused on the process of decolonization.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:45.010
In fact, UN resolution 2065 invited Argentina
and the UK to clear up the matter of the Falklands’

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:46.010
sovereignty in 1965.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:51.080
But within just two years of this resolution,
the situation had taken a 180-degree turn.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:55.890
In 1967 London told the Argentinian government
that it was willing to cede sovereignty over

00:08:55.890 --> 00:09:00.680
the Falklands, but on one condition: they
had to respect the wishes of the kelpers,

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.970
or Falkland Island inhabitants.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.960
The only problem was that the kelpers opposed
any sovereignty transfer point blank.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.170
In fact, they actively mobilized against the
possibility by creating what became known

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:13.480
as the “Falklands Lobby”.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.850
And it seems that at the time, the British
government was willing to deliver, or at least

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:23.060
share, sovereignty over the Falklands in exchange
for commercial agreements.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:28.080
Let me stress here that it was the islands’
inhabitants themselves who prevented this:

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:33.580
( “In the mid-1960s pragmatism would have
probably led the British government to transfer

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:38.670
the sovereignty of the Falklands had not the
Falklands Lobby arisen to counter the pressure

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.330
of Argentine demands”.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:45.400
Clive Ellerby, an English researcher, author
of the work “Role of the Falklands Lobby”.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.540
And that is how things stood on April 2nd,
1982.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:10:00.990
(“May the world know, and America, of a
people with a will, of the Argentine people:

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:11.290
if they want to come, let them, we’ll fight
them.”)

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:19.000
On that day, a grim military government led
by General Leopoldo Galtieri held power in

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Argentina.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.700
It was a discredited and repressive regime,
without the people’s support and responsible

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.920
for one of the darkest periods in Argentina’s
modern history.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:31.520
Many citizens were branded political prisoners,
thousands more simply disappeared, and a huge

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.030
economic crisis was crippling the economy.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:37.190
On that day, Galtieri’s government made
a fateful decision:

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:40.750
Gain the people’s support by feeding their
patriotic fervour.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:49.570
The Argentinian government decided to invade
and occupy the Falkland Islands.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:53.342
The Argentinian authorities expected this
move to spur a diplomatic agreement to be

00:10:53.342 --> 00:10:54.342
reached rapidly with the UK.

00:10:54.342 --> 00:11:02.570
However, they underestimated this woman's
determination:

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:06.330
Margaret Thatcher, the Prime Minister of the
United Kingdom, was willing to fight a war

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:08.260
thousands of kilometres away.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:37.480
The outcome was disastrous.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:41.160
The British army’s technological and military
superiority, coupled with the Argentinian

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:45.140
government’s lack of planning - they carried
out the invasion with many young, poorly equipped

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:48.620
and poorly trained soldiers - allowed the
United Kingdom to recover the islands in a

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:49.830
little over two months.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:54.930
So once again, a tyrannical government’s
political decision ended with a war as unnecessary

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:59.950
as it was useless, costing the lives of hundreds
of people.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:04.920
Look, we know how sensitive these types of
border issues can be.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:09.570
And with this video we want to put some facts
on the table and maybe give you a new perspective:

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:13.260
Because throughout history, territories have
passed from one power to another.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:17.490
For more than 300 years what is now Argentina
was under Spanish rule, for more than 180

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:19.400
years the Falklands have been British.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.430
But what does it matter who was or wasn’t
right 200 years ago?

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:26.510
Isn’t it more important to think about where
we are today and to build a more harmonious

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:27.510
future?

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:28.910
Just a reflection from VisualPolitik.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:33.610
Now, here are some more questions; Why is
there so much interest in the Falklands?

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:35.620
What’s special about these islands?

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:39.070
These are topics we’re going to address
in an upcoming video here on VisualPolitik.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:43.300
But before we go, we’d really like to hear
your thoughts: What do you think should happen

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:44.300
with the Falklands?

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.750
Leave your answers in the comments as well
as in the survey.

